WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.030
<v Maya>Hi everyone, and welcome to this study series on Scheduling.

2
00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:05.250
<v Maya>I'm Maya.

3
00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:06.600
<v Dev>And I'm Dev.

4
00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:13.450
<v Dev>This is the topic that ties the whole personal training course together, so I'm a bit nervous and a bit excited.

5
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:14.940
<v Maya>That is the right mix.

6
00:00:14.990 --> 00:00:17.150
<v Maya>The idea of this series is simple.

7
00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:21.800
<v Maya>Put your headphones on, and by the end you understand scheduling, not just remember it.

8
00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:26.640
<v Maya>I explain, Dev asks the questions you would ask, and we stop to recap often.

9
00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:28.310
<v Dev>So what's the plan?

10
00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:29.560
<v Maya>Six parts.

11
00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:34.780
<v Maya>Parts one to three cover the first scheduling lecture, which is about collecting the ingredients.

12
00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:36.240
<v Maya>Part one is today.

13
00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:39.250
<v Maya>What scheduling actually is, and its objectives.

14
00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:45.040
<v Maya>Part two is how often a muscle can be trained, the rest day, and macro versus micro programming.

15
00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:52.340
<v Maya>Part three is the micro programme of a single session, the seven fundamentals, and the four pillars called FITT.

16
00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:53.930
<v Dev>And the other three?

17
00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:58.120
<v Maya>Parts four to six cover the second lecture, where we finally cook the dish.

18
00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:01.220
<v Maya>Part four is the workout splits and the master table.

19
00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:07.610
<v Maya>Part five is the research on stimulus per set and stimulus retention, which explains how many sets you need.

20
00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:15.570
<v Maya>Part six is hypertrophy and atrophy, the ideal set ranges, the sample programmes, and a rapid fire quiz on everything.

21
00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:16.820
<v Dev>Cooking again.

22
00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:18.650
<v Dev>Is that the analogy?

23
00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:20.790
<v Maya>Yes, and we will meet it in a second.

24
00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:21.730
<v Maya>Let's begin.

25
00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:24.870
<v Maya>Picture designing a workout split like cooking a dish.

26
00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.210
<v Maya>The first thing you need is ingredients.

27
00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:30.330
<v Maya>The first lecture is about arranging the ingredients.

28
00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:32.710
<v Maya>The second lecture prepares the dish.

29
00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:36.070
<v Dev>So some ingredients are things we already learned?

30
00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:36.970
<v Maya>Right.

31
00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:42.140
<v Maya>Some come from earlier lectures, and some are new, based on research from trusted institutes.

32
00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:46.990
<v Maya>Each new topic is another aisle in the shop where you pick up one more ingredient.

33
00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:48.040
<v Dev>Okay.

34
00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:49.830
<v Dev>So, what is scheduling?

35
00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:53.050
<v Maya>Good question, because there is no official definition.

36
00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.740
<v Maya>The class was asked for words that come to mind.

37
00:01:55.790 --> 00:02:00.250
<v Maya>Planning, timings, activities, assigning a task, organising things.

38
00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:01.410
<v Maya>All correct.

39
00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:04.650
<v Maya>The course merges them into one working statement.

40
00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:09.360
<v Maya>Scheduling is organising things in a way that the action leads to results.

41
00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:11.210
<v Dev>And in training terms?

42
00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:14.220
<v Maya>Scheduling is about what, when and how to train.

43
00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:15.630
<v Maya>But that is not all.

44
00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:19.790
<v Maya>You must also keep in mind the body's natural rhythm of three things.

45
00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:22.250
<v Maya>Progress, recovery and fatigue.

46
00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:24.750
<v Dev>Is scheduling the same as programming?

47
00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:26.220
<v Dev>I hear both.

48
00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:27.710
<v Maya>In this course, yes.

49
00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:30.570
<v Maya>Programming and scheduling mean the same thing.

50
00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:33.960
<v Dev>But why isn't it just putting exercises in order?

51
00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:38.430
<v Maya>Because it is also about the things that make the whats, hows and whens successful.

52
00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:39.710
<v Maya>Two examples.

53
00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:46.290
<v Maya>A student with a study timetable knows how much time every topic gets, and the knowledge stays for a long time.

54
00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:52.260
<v Maya>The student with no timetable panics and crams in the last days, and it is gone after the exam.

55
00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:53.810
<v Dev>And the second one?

56
00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:55.520
<v Maya>A doctor's prescription.

57
00:02:55.570 --> 00:03:00.650
<v Maya>This medicine before breakfast, this one with lunch, this one before bed, with exact doses.

58
00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:04.470
<v Maya>Imagine a patient says, why wait, I will take everything at once.

59
00:03:04.520 --> 00:03:07.650
<v Maya>The medicines may not work, or they may even be harmful.

60
00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:09.860
<v Maya>There was a reason for that schedule.

61
00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:13.680
<v Dev>So a schedule needs a logic behind every placement.

62
00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:14.820
<v Maya>Exactly.

63
00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:17.600
<v Maya>Not just some time, some day and some activity.

64
00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:19.050
<v Maya>And one more point.

65
00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:20.970
<v Maya>Take three different clients.

66
00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:23.320
<v Maya>None of them progresses the same way.

67
00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:25.290
<v Maya>None recovers the same way.

68
00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:27.390
<v Maya>None gets fatigued the same way.

69
00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.870
<v Maya>So a schedule must be personalised.

70
00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:33.840
<v Dev>Is there a way to link what, when and how to the rest of the course?

71
00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:38.240
<v Maya>As a study aid, yes, but note the lecture did not draw this line itself.

72
00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:41.220
<v Maya>What to train is answered by the pillar called type.

73
00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:43.750
<v Maya>When to train is answered by frequency.

74
00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:49.590
<v Maya>How to train is answered by intensity and time, plus two finishing touches, sequence and volume.

75
00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:51.920
<v Maya>We will meet all of these in part three.

76
00:03:51.970 --> 00:03:54.350
<v Dev>And one more principle I remember.

77
00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.260
<v Dev>Why before what and how.

78
00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:57.150
<v Maya>Yes.

79
00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:59.620
<v Maya>That is why we start with the objectives.

80
00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:01.640
<v Maya>What is the purpose of a schedule?

81
00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:02.820
<v Maya>Quick recap.

82
00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:05.540
<v Maya>Scheduling and programming mean the same thing.

83
00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:11.090
<v Maya>Scheduling is about what, when and how to train, respecting progress, recovery and fatigue.

84
00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:14.720
<v Maya>It needs a logic, like a study timetable or a prescription.

85
00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:16.660
<v Maya>And it must be personalised.

86
00:04:16.710 --> 00:04:17.970
<v Dev>What, when, how.

87
00:04:18.020 --> 00:04:20.160
<v Dev>Progress, recovery, fatigue.

88
00:04:20.210 --> 00:04:20.950
<v Dev>Got it.

89
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.760
<v Maya>There are four objectives.

90
00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:27.350
<v Maya>Linear progression, be comprehensive, minimum overlap, and avoid overtraining.

91
00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:29.110
<v Maya>Objective one first.

92
00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:30.700
<v Maya>Linear progression.

93
00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:32.880
<v Dev>Linear meaning a straight line?

94
00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:36.010
<v Maya>A steady, uniform, consistent upward trend.

95
00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:38.490
<v Maya>It might be slow, but there is no decline.

96
00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:39.920
<v Maya>Imagine a graph.

97
00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:44.590
<v Maya>Time runs along the bottom, and performance towards your goal runs up the side.

98
00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:51.720
<v Maya>With a schedule, the line climbs steadily, because the schedule respects how the person progresses, recovers and fatigues.

99
00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:53.460
<v Dev>And without a schedule?

100
00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:56.570
<v Maya>The lecture tells it as a story about the gym calendar.

101
00:04:56.620 --> 00:04:59.480
<v Maya>January, new year, new me, and the graph shoots up.

102
00:04:59.530 --> 00:05:02.200
<v Maya>Then February, a breakup, and it drops.

103
00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:06.190
<v Maya>Then the playlist changes, the person is back, motivation returns.

104
00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:09.650
<v Maya>Then a festival in March ruins the diet, and it falls again.

105
00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:12.320
<v Maya>Then a family wedding, so they are back on track.

106
00:05:12.370 --> 00:05:15.290
<v Maya>They are driven by the calendar, not by a schedule.

107
00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:17.100
<v Dev>And friends too, right?

108
00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:17.990
<v Maya>Yes.

109
00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:23.000
<v Maya>A friend says, I feel like doing shoulders today, even though you trained shoulders yesterday.

110
00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:24.080
<v Maya>You do it.

111
00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:27.500
<v Maya>That is growing fatigue and a deviation from your own plan.

112
00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:29.370
<v Maya>The graph becomes a zigzag.

113
00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:34.970
<v Maya>That is non linear progression, and it brings inconsistency, fatigue, injury and a plateau.

114
00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:37.240
<v Dev>What exactly is a plateau?

115
00:05:37.290 --> 00:05:38.440
<v Maya>A flat line.

116
00:05:38.490 --> 00:05:42.960
<v Maya>Think of a hospital film scene where the doctor says the patient has flat lined.

117
00:05:43.010 --> 00:05:44.290
<v Maya>Progress stalls.

118
00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:45.920
<v Maya>So remember the picture.

119
00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:48.330
<v Maya>Linear is a steady staircase going up.

120
00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:51.500
<v Maya>Non linear is a zigzag, and it often ends flat.

121
00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:54.270
<v Dev>Objective two is be comprehensive.

122
00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:57.720
<v Maya>Comprehensive means a schedule leaves no goal behind.

123
00:05:57.770 --> 00:06:00.930
<v Maya>All five components of fitness get equal focus.

124
00:06:00.980 --> 00:06:02.620
<v Maya>Can you name them, Dev?

125
00:06:02.670 --> 00:06:12.350
<v Dev>Cardiorespiratory endurance, muscular endurance, ideal body composition, flexibility and musculoskeletal strength.

126
00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:13.370
<v Maya>Perfect.

127
00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:15.870
<v Maya>A small correction a student once needed.

128
00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:19.100
<v Maya>Say cardiorespiratory, not cardiovascular.

129
00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:24.720
<v Maya>Cardiovascular is a system, and the component is cardiorespiratory endurance.

130
00:06:24.770 --> 00:06:28.240
<v Maya>The word comprehensive also appeared in the cardio lecture.

131
00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:32.400
<v Maya>Task specific muscular endurance is specific to a single task.

132
00:06:32.450 --> 00:06:36.660
<v Maya>Comprehensive is the complete, three hundred and sixty degree picture.

133
00:06:36.710 --> 00:06:39.380
<v Dev>Why must every component be included?

134
00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:41.760
<v Dev>Can't a strength client just lift?

135
00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:44.180
<v Maya>Because the components depend on each other.

136
00:06:44.230 --> 00:06:47.490
<v Maya>If you ignore stretching, flexibility is underdeveloped.

137
00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:51.490
<v Maya>Then you have a limited range of motion and a higher injury chance.

138
00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:55.560
<v Maya>With a limited range, you cannot complete the full range in strength training.

139
00:06:55.610 --> 00:06:58.240
<v Maya>An incomplete range hurts hypertrophy.

140
00:06:58.290 --> 00:07:00.020
<v Maya>Everything is connected.

141
00:07:00.070 --> 00:07:01.560
<v Dev>A chain reaction.

142
00:07:01.610 --> 00:07:05.580
<v Dev>Stretching, flexibility, range of motion, hypertrophy.

143
00:07:05.630 --> 00:07:08.000
<v Maya>And it is not only the one hour in the gym.

144
00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:10.200
<v Maya>It is the other twenty three hours.

145
00:07:10.250 --> 00:07:11.400
<v Maya>Pause and think.

146
00:07:11.450 --> 00:07:16.630
<v Maya>Besides progressive overload, what are the other two legs of the hypertrophy tripod?

147
00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:19.380
<v Dev>Nutrition, and rest and recovery.

148
00:07:19.430 --> 00:07:20.270
<v Maya>Yes.

149
00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:25.670
<v Maya>All three legs matter, and clients will blame the trainer if the other twenty three hours are ignored.

150
00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:28.060
<v Maya>Objectives three and four sound similar.

151
00:07:28.110 --> 00:07:30.650
<v Maya>Minimum overlap and avoid overtraining.

152
00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:32.590
<v Maya>Both have the little word over.

153
00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:34.410
<v Maya>Dev, what is overlap?

154
00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:36.990
<v Dev>Maybe one thing on top of another?

155
00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:39.890
<v Dev>Like training the same muscle repeatedly.

156
00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:41.020
<v Maya>Exactly.

157
00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:46.120
<v Maya>Overlap means the same muscle is trained again with no recovery period in between.

158
00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:49.080
<v Maya>Shoulders on Monday and shoulders again on Tuesday.

159
00:07:49.130 --> 00:07:53.650
<v Maya>If you never train shoulders again that week, it is not necessarily overtraining.

160
00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:56.940
<v Maya>But there was no room for recovery between the two sessions.

161
00:07:56.990 --> 00:07:58.470
<v Maya>That is overlap.

162
00:07:58.520 --> 00:08:00.100
<v Dev>And overtraining?

163
00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:02.140
<v Maya>Doing more than what the muscle needs.

164
00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:04.000
<v Maya>And it can happen in two ways.

165
00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:07.080
<v Maya>Too much volume, which means too many sets and reps.

166
00:08:07.130 --> 00:08:11.630
<v Maya>Or too much frequency, which means training the muscle too many times in a week.

167
00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:13.990
<v Dev>So overlap is about no gap.

168
00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:16.470
<v Dev>Overtraining is about too much.

169
00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:17.360
<v Maya>Yes.

170
00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:18.590
<v Maya>Now volume.

171
00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:21.200
<v Maya>Volume is sets multiplied by reps.

172
00:08:21.250 --> 00:08:25.350
<v Maya>A popular programme writes five by five, five sets of five reps.

173
00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:28.760
<v Maya>Written twelve by three means three sets of twelve reps.

174
00:08:28.810 --> 00:08:30.930
<v Dev>So how many reps should we do?

175
00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:33.900
<v Maya>The course does not just follow ready made programmes.

176
00:08:33.950 --> 00:08:36.150
<v Maya>Earlier you learned stimulating reps.

177
00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:42.100
<v Maya>That is the range of four to six RM, which means a weight you can lift only four to six times.

178
00:08:42.150 --> 00:08:49.150
<v Maya>Here motor unit recruitment is high, tension is high, type two fibres work and the large motor units are recruited.

179
00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:51.160
<v Dev>Is that for everybody?

180
00:08:51.210 --> 00:08:52.000
<v Maya>No.

181
00:08:52.050 --> 00:08:55.750
<v Maya>Stimulating reps are for intermediate and advanced trainees.

182
00:08:55.800 --> 00:09:03.270
<v Maya>Beginners use fifteen to twenty RM, and the recommended reps are about ten to fifteen, because they are in a learning phase.

183
00:09:03.320 --> 00:09:07.030
<v Dev>Hmm, but I remember the slides showing different ranges.

184
00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:08.170
<v Maya>Good catch.

185
00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:13.500
<v Maya>The slides show intermediate at ten to fifteen RM and advanced at six to eight RM.

186
00:09:13.550 --> 00:09:17.730
<v Maya>The lecture, described as the updated version, teaches four to six.

187
00:09:17.780 --> 00:09:21.310
<v Maya>So these notes follow the lecture, but check your course material.

188
00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:22.910
<v Dev>And how many sets?

189
00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:24.750
<v Maya>That cannot be answered yet.

190
00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:27.350
<v Maya>All ingredients must be collected first.

191
00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:30.240
<v Maya>The second lecture gives published data on sets.

192
00:09:30.290 --> 00:09:34.050
<v Maya>For now, volume is kept in check by using stimulating reps.

193
00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:36.600
<v Maya>Let's close with the five things to remember.

194
00:09:36.650 --> 00:09:44.390
<v Maya>One, scheduling is about what, when and how to train, respecting progress, recovery and fatigue, and it must be personalised.

195
00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:46.600
<v Dev>Two, there are four objectives.

196
00:09:46.650 --> 00:09:51.430
<v Dev>Linear progression, comprehensive, minimum overlap, avoid overtraining.

197
00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:53.640
<v Maya>Three, linear is steady and upward.

198
00:09:53.690 --> 00:09:56.670
<v Maya>Without a schedule you get a zigzag and a plateau.

199
00:09:56.720 --> 00:10:03.240
<v Maya>Four, comprehensive means equal focus on all five components of fitness, because they depend on each other.

200
00:10:03.290 --> 00:10:07.500
<v Dev>And five, overlap is the same muscle again with no recovery.

201
00:10:07.550 --> 00:10:11.940
<v Dev>Overtraining is doing more than needed, through volume or frequency.

202
00:10:11.990 --> 00:10:17.570
<v Dev>Volume is sets times reps, with four to six RM for intermediate and advanced.

203
00:10:17.620 --> 00:10:22.750
<v Maya>Next time, we answer how often a muscle can be trained, using a table of sets and recovery.

